  LANGUAGE = {en},
  ABSTRACT = {Crowdfunding has mostly been used to ﬁnance very unique projects. Recently, however, companies have begun using it to ﬁnance more traditional products where they compete against other sellers of similar products. Major crowdfunding platforms, Kickstarter and Indiegogo, as well as Amazon have launched several projects consistent with this trend. This paper offers a model where two competing ﬁrms can use crowdfunding prior to direct sales. The model provides several implications that have not yet been tested e.g., (1) Firms can use crowdfunding strategically to signal a high level of demand for their products; (2) (Reward-based) crowdfunding is procyclical; (3) A higher platform fee may lead to higher ﬁrm proﬁts in equilibrium; (4) Competition increases the chances of using crowdfunding compared to the monopoly case; (5) A non-monotonic relationship exists between the risk of crowdfunding campaign failure and ﬁrm proﬁt.},
  URL = {https://www.mdpi.com/1911-8074/13/3/39},
  URLDATE = {2023-10-26},
