  SHORTTITLE = {The {New} {Black}},
  FILE = {Full Text PDF:/Users/corywhitney/Zotero/storage/648GMS76/Munoz - 2019 - The New Black Third-Party Litigation Funding.pdf:application/pdf},
  LANGUAGE = {en\_US},
  ABSTRACT = {Litigation finance is not new. The fact that some of today’s ethical restrictions on legal fee arrangements date back to Medieval England tell us that people have been trying to figure out creative ways to pay for lawsuits for at least that long. Leading up to the early 2000s, litigation finance existed in various forms, from factoring to claims trading in bankruptcy, and of course the contingency fee arrangement. Under this common fee structure, plaintiffs’ lawyers essentially act as both legal counsel and financier by extending firm resources to prosecute claims in return for a portion of the recovery. The practice of monetizing those contingent claims, and viewing them as an investment-worthy asset class, has captured the imagination of entrepreneurs, legislators, and legal reform advocates. Some suggest that litigation finance will revolutionize the legal industry, and spur innovation, by changing how cashflows from legal claims can defray and redistribute the cost of litigation. Indeed, litigation finance is an important tool that companies can use to prosecute valuable claims and reduce the cost of defense litigation. Today, litigation finance seems to be the new black. It is important for policy makers and business people to understand its history, limits, and application in order to properly make use of the asset and develop a sensible regulatory structure that maintains access to the court system.},
